{"tool_name":"hca","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"hca","identifiers":["biotools:hca","doi:10.1007/s000180050082"],"license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"Human cell atlas data storage system command line interface. this repository is a command line interface (cli) and python library (api) for interacting with the data coordination platform (dcp) of the human cell atlas (hca). currently the hca package supports interaction with the upload service and data storage service (dss) for services such as uploading, downloading, and querying data.","home_url":"https://github.com/HumanCellAtlas/dcp-cli","keywords":["Amino Acid Sequence","Chemical Phenomena","Chemistry, Physical","Humans","Models, Molecular","Molecular Sequence Data","Molecular Structure","Proteins","Sequence Analysis","alpha 1-Antitrypsin","Proteomics","Protein secondary structure"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"hca","home":"http://mobyle.rpbs.univ-paris-diderot.fr/cgi-bin/portal.py?form=HCA#forms::HCA","summary":"The HCA method is based on the use of a bidimensional plot, called the HCA plot. The bidimensional plot is associated with an alpha helicoidal pitch (3.6 residue/turn, connectivity distance of 4) which has been shown to offer the best correspondence between clusters and regular secondary structures. Examination of the HCA plot of a protein sequence allow to easily identify globular regions from non globular ones and, in globular regions, to identify secondary structures.","addition_date":"2017-12-06T18:14:33Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T15:47:21.770746Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"collections":["Proteomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:hca","credit":[{"email":"Isabelle.Callebaut@lmcp.jussieu.fr","name":"Isabelle Callebaut","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://bioserv.rpbs.univ-paris-diderot.fr/services/HCA/"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["daniela.mereuta","proteomics.bio.tools"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Protein hydrophobic region calculation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0405"}]}],"name":"HCA (hydrophobic cluster analysis)","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"admin","publication":[{"doi":"10.1007/s000180050082","metadata":{"abstract":"Tell years after the idea of hydrophobic cluster analysis (HCA) was conceived and first published, theoretical and practical experience has shown this unconventional method of protein sequence analysis to be particularly efficient and sensitive, especially with families of sequences sharing low levels of sequence identity. This extreme sensitivity has made it possible to predict the functions of genes whose sequence similarities are hardly if at all detectable by current one-dimensional (1D) methods alone, and offers a new way to explore the enormous amount of data generated by genome sequencing. HCA also provides original tools to understand fundamental features of protein stability and folding. Since the last review of HCA published in 1990 [1], significant improvements have been made and several new facets have been addressed. Here we wish to update and summarize this information.","authors":[{"name":"Callebaut I."},{"name":"Canard L."},{"name":"Chomilier J."},{"name":"Durand P."},{"name":"Henrissat B."},{"name":"Labesse G."},{"name":"Mornon J.P."},{"name":"Poupon A."}],"citationCount":444,"date":"1997-11-05T00:00:00Z","journal":"Cellular and Molecular Life Sciences","title":"Deciphering protein sequence information through hydrophobic cluster analysis (HCA): Current status and perspectives"},"pmcid":"PMC11147222","pmid":"9351466","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Protein secondary structure","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3542"},{"term":"Proteomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0121"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"HCA (hydrophobic cluster analysis)","home":"https://bio.tools/hca","summary":"The HCA method is based on the use of a bidimensional plot, called the HCA plot. The bidimensional plot is associated with an alpha helicoidal pitch (3.6 residue/turn, connectivity distance of 4) which has been shown to offer the best correspondence between clusters and regular secondary structures. Examination of the HCA plot of a protein sequence allow to easily identify globular regions from non globular ones and, in globular regions, to identify secondary structures.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:hca/web/mobyle.rpbs.univ-paris-diderot.fr","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":156,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2025"},{"count":1,"year":"2026"},{"count":2,"year":"-1"},{"count":2,"year":"2012"},{"count":2,"year":"2020"},{"count":2,"year":"2024"},{"count":3,"year":"1998"},{"count":3,"year":"2006"},{"count":3,"year":"2011"},{"count":4,"year":"2000"},{"count":4,"year":"2004"},{"count":4,"year":"2022"},{"count":5,"year":"1999"},{"count":5,"year":"2002"},{"count":5,"year":"2016"},{"count":5,"year":"2019"},{"count":5,"year":"2023"},{"count":6,"year":"2003"},{"count":6,"year":"2013"},{"count":6,"year":"2015"},{"count":6,"year":"2017"},{"count":6,"year":"2018"},{"count":7,"year":"2001"},{"count":7,"year":"2014"},{"count":7,"year":"2021"},{"count":8,"year":"2010"},{"count":9,"year":"2007"},{"count":10,"year":"2008"},{"count":10,"year":"2009"},{"count":12,"year":"2005"}],"doi":"10.1007/s000180050082","pmcid":"PMC11147222","pmid":"9351466","ref_count":0,"refs":[],"title":"Deciphering protein sequence information through hydrophobic cluster analysis (HCA): current status and perspectives.","year":1997}]}],"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright":false,"https"